{'role': 'user', 'content': '**Task Instruction:**\n\nYou are tasked with reading and analyzing web pages based on the following inputs: **Previous Reasoning Steps**, **Current Search Query**, and **Searched Web Pages**. Your objective is to extract relevant and helpful information for **Current Search Query** from the **Searched Web Pages** and seamlessly integrate this information into the **Previous Reasoning Steps** to continue reasoning for the original question.\n\n**Guidelines:**\n\n1. **Analyze the Searched Web Pages:**\n- Carefully review the content of each searched web page.\n- Identify factual information that is relevant to the **Current Search Query** and can aid in the reasoning process for the original question.\n\n2. **Extract Relevant Information:**\n- Select the information from the Searched Web Pages that directly contributes to advancing the **Previous Reasoning Steps**.\n- Ensure that the extracted information is accurate and relevant.\n\n3. **Output Format:**\n- Present the helpful information for current search query: beginning with `**Final Information**` as shown below.\n**Final Information**\n\n[Helpful information]\n\n**Inputs:**\n- **Previous Reasoning Steps:**  \nStep 1: Okay, the user is asking why people are afraid of dying. This is a deep, philosophical question that touches on psychology, biology, and even cultural aspects. Let me start by recalling what I know about this topic.\n\n...\n\nStep 6: Wait, the user mentioned using the special tools for web searches. So I need to structure the search query properly. Let me write the search query as specified.\n\nStep 7: First search query: "why do humans fear death psychological reasons"\n\nStep 8: Wait, but the exact format should be between <|begin_search_query|> and <|end_search_query|>\n\n- **Current Search Query:**  \nand\n\n- **Searched Web Pages:**  \n**Web Page 1:**\n{\n  "context": "And - definition of and by The Free Dictionary\\nand\\nAlso found in: Thesaurus, Medical, Legal, Financial, Acronyms, Idioms, Encyclopedia, Wikipedia .\\nAND\\n(ănd)\\nn.\\nA logical operator that returns a true value only if both operands are true.\\n[From and .]\\nand\\n(ənd, ən; ănd when stressed)\\nconj.\\n1. Together with or along with; in addition to; as well as. Used to connect words, phrases, or clauses that have the same grammatical function in a construction.\\n2. Added to; plus: Two and two makes four.\\n3. Used to indicate result: Give the boy a chance, and he might surprise you.\\n4. Informal Used after a verb such as come, go, or try to introduce another verb describing the purpose of the action: come and see; try and find it. See Usage Note at try.\\n5. Archaic If: and it please you.\\nn.\\nAn addition or stipulation: The offer is final—no ifs, ands, or buts.\\nIdioms:\\nand so forth /on\\n1. And other unspecified things of the same class: bought groceries, went to the bank, picked up the dry cleaning, and so forth.\\n2. Further in the same manner.\\nand then some Informal\\nWith considerably more in addition: This project will take all our skill and then some.\\n[Middle English, from Old English; see en in Indo-European roots .]\\nUsage Note: A traditional grammatical rule asserts that sentences beginning with and or but express \\"incomplete thoughts\\" and are therefore incorrect. But this stricture has been ignored by writers from Shakespeare to Joyce Carol Oates, and most of the Usage Panel sees wisdom in this attitude. In our 1988 survey, when asked whether they paid attention to the rule in their own writing, 24 percent answered \\"always or usually,\\" 36 percent answered \\"sometimes,\\" and 40 percent answered \\"rarely or never.\\" See Usage Notes at both, but, with.\\nAmerican Heritage® Dictionary of the English Language, Fifth Edition. Copyright © 2016 by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt Publishing Company. Published by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt Publishing Company. All rights reserved.\\nand\\n(ænd; unstressed ənd; ən)\\nconj (coordinating)\\n1. along with; in addition to: boys and girls.\\n2. as a consequence: he fell down and cut his knee.\\n3. afterwards: we pay the man and go through that door.\\n4. (preceded by: good or nice)(intensifier): the sauce is good and thick.\\n5. plus: two and two equals four.\\n6. used to join identical words or phrases to give emphasis or indicate repetition or continuity: better and better; we ran and ran; it rained and rained.\\n7. used to join two identical words or phrases to express a contrast between instances of what is named: there are jobs and jobs.\\n8. informal used in place of to in infinitives after verbs such as try, go, and come: try and see it my way.\\n9. an obsolete word for if: and it please you.Informal spellings: an, an\' or \'n\\nn\\n(usually plural)an additional matter or problem: ifs, ands, or buts.\\n[Old English and; related to Old Frisian anda, Old Saxon ande, Old High German anti, Sanskrit atha]\\nUsage: The use of and instead of to after try and wait is typical of spoken language, but should be avoided in any writing which is not informal: We must try to prevent (not try and prevent) this happening\\nAND\\nabbreviation for\\n(Placename) Andorra (international car registration)\\nCollins English Dictionary – Complete and Unabridged, 12th Edition 2014 © HarperCollins Publishers 1991, 1994, 1998, 2000, 2003, 2006, 2007, 2009, 2011, 2014\\nand\\n(and; unstressed ənd, ən, or, esp. after a homorganic consonant, n),\\nconj.\\n1. (used to connect grammatically coordinate words, phrases, or clauses) with; as well as; in addition to: pens and pencils.\\n2. added to; plus: 2 and 2 are 4.\\n3. then: He finished and went to bed.\\n4. also, at the same time: to sleep and dream.\\n5. (used to imply different qualities in things having the same name): There are bargains and bargains, so watch out.\\n6. (used to introduce a sentence, implying continuation) also; then: And he said unto Moses.\\n7. Informal. to (used between two finite verbs): Try and do it.\\n8. (used to introduce a consequence or conditional result): Say one more word and I\'ll scream.\\n9. but; on the contrary: He tried to run five miles and couldn\'t.\\n10. Archaic. if: and you please. Compare an 3.\\nn.\\n11. an added condition, stipulation, or particular: no ands or buts about it.\\n12. Logic. the connective used in conjunction.\\nIdioms:\\nand so forth or so on, and the like; and more of the same; et cetera.\\n[before 900; Middle English; Old English and, ond; c. Old Saxon, Old High German ant, Old Frisian, Gothic and, Icelandic and-; akin to German und, Dutch en, Skt anti]\\nusage: Both and and but,and to a lesser extent or and so,are common as transitional words at the beginnings of sentences in all types of speech and writing: It grew dark as clouds filled the sky. And then the rains began. Any objection to this practice probably stems from the overuse of such sentences by inexperienced writers. See also and/or, et cetera, try.\\nAND\\n(ænd)\\nn.\\na Boolean operator that returns a positive result when both operands are positive.\\n[1945–50]\\nRandom House Kernerman Webster\'s College Dictionary, © 2010 K Dictionaries Ltd. Copyright 2005, 1997, 1991 by Random House, Inc. All rights reserved.\\nand\\nAnd can be used to link noun phrases, adjectives, adverbs, verbs, or clauses.\\n1. used for linking noun phrases\\nWhen you are talking about two things or people, you put and between two noun phrases.\\nI had a cup of tea and a biscuit.\\nThe story is about a friendship between a boy and a girl.\\nWhen you are linking more than two noun phrases, you usually only put and in front of the last one.\\nThey had fish, potatoes, and peas for dinner.\\nWe need to build more roads, bridges and airports.\\nIn lists like these, the comma before and is optional.\\n2. used for linking adjectives\\nYou put and between two adjectives when they come after linking verbs such as be, seem,and feel.\\nThe room was large and square.\\nShe felt cold and tired.\\nWhen there are more than two adjectives after a linking verb, you usually only put and in front of the last one.\\nWe felt hot, tired, and thirsty.\\nThe child is outgoing, happy and busy.\\nIn lists like these, the comma before and is optional.\\nWhen you use two or more adjectives in front of a noun, you don\'t usually put and between them.\\nShe was wearing a beautiful pink dress.\\nWe made rapid technological advance.\\nHowever, if the adjectives are colour adjectives, you must use and.\\nI bought a black and white swimming suit.\\nSimilarly, if you are using adjectives that classify a noun in a similar way, you use and.\\nThis is a social and educational dilemma.\\nYou also use and when you put adjectives in front of a plural noun in order to talk about groups of things that have different or opposite qualities.\\nBoth large and small firms deal with each other regularly.\\nBe Careful!\\nDon\'t use \'and\' to link adjectives when you want them to contrast with each other. For example, don\'t say\'We were tired and happy \'. You say \'We were tired but happy\'.\\nThey stayed in a small but comfortable hotel.\\n3. used for linking adverbs\\nYou can use and to link adverbs.\\nMary was breathing quietly and evenly.\\nThey walked up and down, smiling.\\n4. used for linking verbs\\nYou use and to link verbs when you are talking about actions performed by the same person, thing, or group.\\nI was shouting and swearing.\\nThey sat and chatted.\\nIf you want to say that someone does something repeatedly or for a long time, you can use and after a verb, and then repeat the verb.\\nThey laughed and laughed.\\nIsaac didn\'t give up. He tried and tried.\\nIn conversation, you can sometimes use and after try or wait instead of using a to -infinitive clause. For example, instead of saying \'I\'ll try to get a newspaper\', you say \'I\'ll try and get a newspaper\'. In sentences like these you are describing one action, not two.\\nI\'ll try and answer the question.\\nI prefer to wait and see how things go.\\nYou only use and like this when you are using a future form of try or wait,or when you are using the infinitive or imperative form.\\nIf you go and do something or come and do something, you move from one place to another in order to do it.\\nI\'ll go and see him in the morning.\\nWould you like to come and stay with us?\\n5. used for linking clauses\\nAnd is often used to link clauses.\\nI came here in 1972 and I have lived here ever since.\\nWhen you are giving advice or a warning, you can use and to say what will happen if something is done. For example, instead of saying \'If you go by train, you\'ll get there quicker\', you can say \'Go by train and you\'ll get there quicker\'.\\nDo as you\'re told and you\'ll be all right.\\nYou can put and at the beginning of a sentence when you are writing down what someone said, or writing in a conversational style.\\nI didn\'t mean to scare you. And I\'m sorry I\'m late.\\n6. leaving out repeated words\\nWhen you are linking verb phrases that contain the same auxiliary verb, you don\'t need to repeat the auxiliary verb.\\nJohn had already showered and changed.\\nSimilarly, when you are linking nouns that have the same adjective, preposition, or determiner in front of them, you don\'t need to repeat the adjective, preposition, or determiner.\\nMy mother and father worked hard.\\n7. \'both\' for emphasis\\nWhen you link two phrases using and,you can emphasize that what you are saying applies to both phrases by putting both in front of the first phrase.\\nThey feel both anxiety and joy.\\nSee both\\n8. negative sentences\\nYou don\'t normally use \'and\' to link groups of words in negative sentences. For example, don\'t say\'She never reads and listens to stories \'. You say \'She never reads or listens to stories\'.\\nHe was not exciting or good looking.\\nSee or\\nHowever, you use and when you are talking about the possibility of two actions happening at the same time. For example, you say \'I can\'t think and talk at the same time\'. You also use and if two noun phrases occur so frequently together that they are regarded as a single item. For example, knife and fork are always joined by and even in negative sentences such as \'I haven\'t got my knife and fork\'.\\nUnions haven\'t taken health and safety seriously.\\nWhen two noun phrases are regarded as a single item like this, they almost always occur in a fixed order. For example, you talk about your knife and fork,not your\'fork and knife \'.\\nCollins COBUILD English Usage © HarperCollins Publishers 1992, 2004, 2011, 2012\\nThesaurus Antonyms Related Words Synonyms Legend:\\nSwitch to new thesaurus\\nand\\nconjunction\\n1. also, including, along with, together with, in addition to, as well as When he returned, she and her boyfriend had already gone.\\n2. moreover, plus, furthermore, besides These airlines fly to isolated places. And business travellers use them.\\nCollins Thesaurus of the English Language – Complete and Unabridged 2nd Edition. 2002 © HarperCollins Publishers 1995, 2002\\nTranslations\\nSpanish / Español\\nSelect a language:\\ny but not hie-) e e (before i- hi\\nand\\n[ænd ənd nd ən] CONJ\\n1. → y; (before i-, hi- but not hie-) → e\\nyou and me → tú y yo\\nFrench and English → francés e inglés\\nand? → ¿y?, ¿y qué más?\\nand how! → ¡y (no veas) cómo!\\nand/or → y/o\\n2. (with compar adj) better and better → cada vez mejor\\nmore and more → cada vez más\\nmore and more difficult → cada vez más difícil\\n3. (in numbers) one and a half → uno y medio\\na hundred and one → ciento uno\\ntwo hundred and ten → doscientos diez\\nfive hours and 20 minutes → cinco horas y 20 minutos\\nten dollars and 50 cents → diez dólares y or con 50 centavos\\n4. (negative sense) → ni\\nwithout shoes and socks → sin zapatos ni calcetines\\nyou can\'t buy and sell here → aquí no se permite comprar ni vender\\n5. (repetition, continuation) she cried and cried → no dejaba de llorar, lloraba sin parar\\nI rang and rang → llamé muchas veces\\nhe talked and talked → habló sin parar or (LAm) cesar\\n6. (before infin) try and do it → trata de hacerlo\\nplease try and come! → ¡ procura venir!\\nwait and see → espera y verás\\ncome and see me → ven a verme\\n7. (implying a distinction) there are lawyers and lawyers! → hay abogados y abogados\\n8. (implying a conditional) one move and you\'re dead! → ¡como te muevas disparo !, ¡un solo movimiento y disparo!\\nAND\\nIn order to avoid two \\"i\\" sounds coming together, and is translated by e not y before words beginning with i and hi and before the letter y used on its own: ...Spain and Italy... ...España e Italia... ...grapes and figs... ...uvas e higos... ...words ending in S and Y... ...palabras terminadas en S e Y...\\nNOTE Words beginning with hie are preceded by y, since hie is not pronounced \\"i\\":\\n...coal and iron mines... ...minas de carbón y hierro...\\nCollins Spanish Dictionary - Complete and Unabridged 8th Edition 2005 © William Collins Sons & Co. Ltd. 1971, 1988 © HarperCollins Publishers 1992, 1993, 1996, 1997, 2000, 2003, 2005\\nand\\n(ənd) , (ӕnd) conjunction\\n1. joining two statements, pieces of information etc. I opened the door and went inside; The hat was blue and red; a mother and child. y\\n2. in addition to. 2 and 2 makes 4. y, más\\n3. as a result of which. Try hard and you will succeed. y\\n4. used instead of `to\' with a verb. Do try and come! y\\nKernerman English Multilingual Dictionary © 2006-2013 K Dictionaries Ltd.\\nand\\n→ y\\nMultilingual Translator © HarperCollins Publishers 2009\\nand\\nconj. y; e (gr. used instead of y before words beginning with i or hi);\\nfather and son → padre e hijo;\\ntwo thirty → las dos y media.\\nEnglish-Spanish Medical Dictionary © Farlex 2012\\nAnd you? → ¿Y usted?\\nIn black and white → En blanco y negro\\nEating (US)\\nEating and Drinking (UK) → La comida y la bebida\\nCollins Multilingual Translator © HarperCollins Publishers 2009\\nWant to thank TFD for its existence? Tell a friend about us, add a link to this page, or visit the webmaster\'s page for free fun content .\\nLink to this page:\\nSite: Follow:\\nFacebook\\nTwitter\\nRss\\nMail\\nShare:\\nFacebook\\nTwitter\\nLinkedIn\\nMail\\nOpen / Close",\n  "url": "https://www.thefreedictionary.com/and"\n}\n**Web Page 2:**\n{\n  "context": "Urban Dictionary: and\\nand\\nA conjunction to link two clauses together.\\nThe dog jumped over the fence AND the cat did too.\\nby Kieran Jones February 26, 2005\\n413\\n157\\nGet the and mug.\\nand\\nto basicly say \\"SO WHAT?\\". when you say and you really juss askin why someone is all in ya BI. *and is said in a smart way.\\nsome chick~ Yea tracie i heard that you was walkin tone to the store last night!\\nTracie~ And?...(and your point is?)\\nAnd?...( what you gon do about it?)\\nAnd?...(finish ya statement!)\\nby Jiggy\'Ma May 17, 2007\\n147\\n53\\nGet the and mug.\\nand\\nListen,\\ntheres nothing i cannot overcome\\nfor love\\nIts my holy place and i know i can fight\\nI just need to know its real\\nIm talking from the heart\\nand my intention makes me fearless\\nby Clothy June 10, 2019\\n16\\n3\\nGet the and mug.\\nand\\nSome crazy word when translated into\\nSpanish = y\\nFrench = et\\nGerman = und\\netc.\\nUsed to link two or more objects which may directly correspond with eachother in the contex or relativity of a sentence.\\nI\'m going to do homework and go to bed now. Bye.\\nby Alex Bo Balex April 19, 2005\\n71\\n38\\nGet the and mug.\\nand\\nWord used by illiterate fucktards in place of the indefinite article \\"an.\\"\\nDuhhh, I\'m and idiot. What do you mean, I\'m \\"an idiot ,\\" not \\"and idiot?\\"  I mean, \\"an\\" isn\'t even a word.   You\'re and asshole.\\nby Beerios May 17, 2005\\n153\\n111\\nGet the and mug.\\nand\\nTo link a phrse or list together. Or when your bored and can\'t think up conversation, an ice breaker you use \'and\'.\\nShe was butters and ugly and disgraceful and fat and she had ketchup and marmite and blood and chocolate down her top.\\nby laurie the funny name......... one January 13, 2006\\n31\\n24\\nFlag\\nGet the and mug.\\nand\\nBoolean logic operator where all values in the argument must be true in order for the whole statement to be true.  Often denoted with \'&\' (\'&&\' in programming languages).\\nIf x=1 and y =0, then\\n(x AND y) = 0\\nby cloudRunner March 22, 2006\\n70\\n64\\nGet the and mug.\\nNext › Last »\\n1 2 3 4 5\\nMore random definitions",\n  "url": "https://www.urbandictionary.com/define.php?term=and"\n}\n**Web Page 3:**\n{\n  "context": "And - English Grammar Today - Cambridge Dictionary\\nAnd\\nGrammar > Words, sentences and clauses > Conjunctions and linking words > And\\nfrom English Grammar Today\\nAnd is a coordinating conjunction. We use and to connect two words, phrases, clauses or prefixes together:\\nTelevisions and computers are dominating our daily life. (noun + noun)\\nI have to shower and change. (verb + verb)\\nThe photos are black and white. (adjective + adjective)\\nMy best friend and my father’s father both come from Wales. (phrase + phrase)\\nShe got to the door and put the key in the latch. (clause + clause)\\nThe houses were a mix of pre- and post-war build. (prefix + prefix)\\nAnd, but, either … or\\nGo and, come and\\nSpoken English:\\nIn informal speaking we often use and after go and come before verbs like ask, buy, check, collect, do, find, get:\\nWhy don’t you go and ask her?\\nI need to go and buy myself some running shoes.\\nCan you come and do the washing up?\\nShe should go and find another job.\\nIn informal American English speaking, we often leave out and:\\nDad will come collect you on the way back from work.\\nI was expecting him to go get the keys.\\nFixed expressions with and\\nWe often use and in common fixed expressions. The order of the words cannot change: peace and quiet, pick and choose, come and go, knife and fork, black and white.\\nAnd + adjective\\nSpoken English:\\nIn informal speaking, when we want to emphasise something positive, we often use and after nice or lovely with another adjective:\\nThe kitchen’s looking nice and clean.\\nYou’re lovely and tanned.\\nAnd in numbers\\nWhen we speak or write numbers, we use and to separate hundred, thousand, million from numbers smaller than a hundred:\\n625: six hundred and twenty-five\\n1,000,410: one million, four hundred and ten\\n6,492: six thousand, four hundred and ninety-two\\n101: one hundred and one\\nWe also use and between whole numbers and fractions:\\n23½: twenty three and a half\\n01 Above\\n06 I work\\n07 as well too\\n09 Look forward to\\n10 Speak talk\\nTo top\\nContents",\n  "url": "https://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/grammar/british-grammar/and"\n}\n**Web Page 4:**\n{\n  "context": "And - English Grammar Today - Cambridge Dictionary\\nAnd\\nGrammar > Words, sentences and clauses > Conjunctions and linking words > And\\nfrom English Grammar Today\\nAnd is a coordinating conjunction. We use and to connect two words, phrases, clauses or prefixes together:\\nTelevisions and computers are dominating our daily life. (noun + noun)\\nI have to shower and change. (verb + verb)\\nThe photos are black and white. (adjective + adjective)\\nMy best friend and my father’s father both come from Wales. (phrase + phrase)\\nShe got to the door and put the key in the latch. (clause + clause)\\nThe houses were a mix of pre- and post-war build. (\\nAnd, but, either … or\\nGo and, come and\\nSpoken English:\\nIn informal speaking we often use and after go and come before verbs like ask, buy, check, collect, do, find, get:\\nWhy don’t you go and ask her?\\nI need to go and buy myself some running shoes.\\nCan you come and do the washing up?\\nShe should go and find another job.\\nIn informal American English speaking, we often leave out and:\\nDad will come collect you on the way back from work.\\nI was expecting him to go get the keys.\\nFixed expressions with and\\nWe often use and in common fixed expressions. The order of the words cannot change: peace and quiet, pick and choose, come and go, knife and fork, black and white.\\nAnd + adjective\\nSpoken English:\\nIn informal speaking, when we want to emphasise something positive, we often use and after nice or lovely with another adjective:\\nThe kitchen’s looking nice and clean.\\nYou’re lovely and tanned.\\nAnd in numbers\\nWhen we speak or write numbers, we use and to separate hundred, thousand, million from numbers smaller than a hundred:\\n625: six hundred and twenty-five\\n1,000,410: one million, four hundred and ten\\n6,492: six thousand, four hundred and ninety-two\\n101: one hundred and one\\nWe also use and between whole numbers and fractions:\\n23½: twenty three and a half\\n01 A/an and\\n03 Adverbs and adverb phrases: position\\n04\\n05 Also as well too\\n06 Present simple (I work\\n07 Pronouns: possessive (my, mine, your, , etc.)\\n08 Present perfect continuous (\\n09 Made from made of made out of made with\\n10 Speak or talk\\nTo top\\nContents",\n  "url": "https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/and"\n}\n**Web Page 5:**\n{\n  "context": "And definition and meaning | Collins English Dictionary\\nand Video pronunciation English: and English: AND English: -and American: and and AND Example sentences Word lists Trends In other languages\\nDefinition of\u2004\'and\'\\nWord Frequency\\nand\\n(ənd, STRONG ænd)\\n1. conjunction\\nYou use and to link two or more words, groups, or clauses .\\nWhen he returned, she and Simon had already gone.\\nBetween 1914 and 1920 large parts of Albania were occupied by the Italians.\\nI\'m going to write good jokes and become a good comedian.\\nI\'m 53 and I\'m very happy.\\nSynonyms: also, including, along with, together with More Synonyms of and\\n2. conjunction\\nYou use and to link two words or phrases that are the same in order to emphasize the degree of something, or to suggest that something continues or increases over a period of time.\\n[emphasis]\\nDay by day I am getting better and better.\\nWe talked for hours and hours.\\nHe lay down on the floor and cried and cried.\\n3. conjunction\\nYou use and to link two statements about events when one of the events follows the other.\\nI waved goodbye and went down the stone harbour steps.\\nI pulled the door closed and did up my boots on the landing.\\n4. conjunction\\nYou use and to link two statements when the second statement continues the point that has been\\nmade in the first statement.\\nYou could only tell the effects of the disease in the long term, and five years wasn\'t\\nlong enough.\\nThe cure for bad teaching is good teachers, and good teachers cost money.\\n5. conjunction\\nYou use and to link two clauses when the second clause is a result of the first clause.\\nAll through the day crowds arrived and by midnight thousands of people packed the\\nsquare.\\n6. conjunction\\nYou use and to interrupt yourself in order to make a comment on what you are saying .\\nAs Downing claims, and as we noted above, reading is best established when the child\\nhas an intimate knowledge of the language.\\nFinally–and I really ought to stop in a minute–I wish to make the following recommendations.\\n7. conjunction\\nYou use and at the beginning of a sentence to introduce something else that you want to add to what you have just said. Some people think that starting a sentence with and is ungrammatical, but it is now quite common in both spoken and written English.\\nCommuter airlines fly to out-of-the-way places. And business travelers are the ones\\nwho go to those locations.\\nSynonyms: moreover, plus, furthermore, besides More Synonyms of and\\n8. conjunction\\nYou use and to introduce a question which follows logically from what someone has just said.\\n\'He used to be so handsome.\'—\'And now?\'\\n\'Well, of course, they haven\'t won a football game.\'—\'And what would you expect?\'\\n9. conjunction\\nAnd is used by broadcasters and people making announcements to change a topic or to start talking about a topic they have just mentioned .\\nAnd now the headlines.\\nFootball, and Aston Villa are playing at home today.\\n10. conjunction\\nYou use and to indicate that two numbers are to be added together.\\nWhat does two and two make?\\n11. conjunction\\nAnd is used before a fraction that comes after a whole number.\\n...spent five and a half years.\\n...fourteen and a quarter per cent.\\n12. conjunction\\nYou use and in numbers larger than one hundred, after the words \'hundred\' or \' thousand\' and before other numbers.\\nWe printed two hundred and fifty invitations.\\n...three thousand and twenty-six pounds.\\nCOBUILD Advanced English Dictionary. Copyright © HarperCollins Publishers\\nEnglish Easy Learning Grammar What are coordinating clauses in English? The process called\xa0coordination\xa0joins two short clauses of equal importance with a\\nconjunction. Each clause becomes a\xa0main\xa0clause in the new sentence.\\nAnn ... Read more\\nVideo: pronunciation of\\nand\\nWord Frequency\\nand in British English\\n(ænd, unstressed ənd, ən)\\nconjunction(coordinating)\\n1.\\nalong with; in addition to\\nboys and girls\\n2.\\nas a consequence\\nhe fell down and cut his knee\\n3.\\nafterwards\\nwe pay the man and go through that door\\n4.(preceded by good or nice)\\n(intensifier )\\nthe sauce is good and thick\\n5.\\nplus\\ntwo and two equals four\\n6.\\nused to join identical words or phrases to give emphasis or indicate repetition or continuity\\nbetter and better\\nwe ran and ran\\nit rained and rained\\n7.\\nused to join two identical words or phrases to express a contrast between instances of what is named\\nthere are jobs and jobs\\n8. informal\\nused in place of to in infinitives after verbs such as try, go, and come\\ntry and see it my way\\n9. an obsolete word for if\\nand it please you\\nInformal spellings: an, an\', \'n\\nnoun\\n10.(usually plural)\\nan additional matter or problem\\nifs, ands, or buts\\n▶\xa0USAGE The use of and instead of to after try and wait is typical of spoken language, but should be avoided in any writing which is not\\ninformal: We must try to prevent (not try and prevent) this happening\\nCollins English Dictionary. Copyright © HarperCollins Publishers\\nWord origin\\nOld English and; related to Old Frisian anda, Old Saxon ande, Old High German anti, Sanskrit atha\\nWord Frequency\\nAND in British English\\nabbreviation for\\nAndorra (international car registration )\\nCollins English Dictionary. Copyright © HarperCollins Publishers\\nWord Frequency\\n-and in British English\\nor -end\\nsuffix forming nouns\\nindicating a person or thing that is to be dealt with in a specified way\\nanalysand\\ndividend\\nmultiplicand\\nCollins English Dictionary. Copyright © HarperCollins Publishers\\nWord origin\\nfrom Latin gerundives ending in -andus, -endus\\nWord Frequency\\nand in American English\\n(ænd; unstressed, ənd; ən; ə n)\\nconjunction\\n1.\\nin addition; also; as well as\\nused to join elements of similar syntactic structure\\napples and pears; a red and white dress; he begged and borrowed\\n2.\\nplus; added to\\n6 and 2 equals 8\\n3.\\nbut; yet; in contrast\\nvegetable oil is digestible and mineral oil is not\\n4.\\nthen again; then in addition\\nused between two instances of the same word to express repetition or continuity\\nwe talked and talked\\n5.\\nas a consequence or result\\nhe told her and she wept\\n6.\\nthen; following this\\nshe drove to the store and bought groceries\\n7. Informal\\nto\\nused as a sign of the infinitive\\ntry and understand\\n8.\\nas well as other kinds of\\nused between two instances of the same word to express difference in kind or quality\\nthere are painters and painters, my friend\\n9. Archaic\\nthen\\nused before a sentence\\nand it came to pass\\n10. Obsolete\\nif\\n▶\xa0USAGE: And is also used correlatively with both, indicating inclusion of all of the items mentioned And is sometimes also used informally as a superfluous element connecting clauses in\\nconversation\\nWebster’s New World College Dictionary, 4th Edition. Copyright © 2010 by\\nHoughton Mifflin Harcourt. All rights reserved.\\nWord origin\\nME and, an < OE and, ond; akin to Ger und, OHG unti, OS endi, ON enn: the orig. meaning was “thereupon, then, next”\\nWord Frequency\\nand in American English\\n(ænd, unstressed ənd, ən, or, esp. after a homorganic consonant n)\\nconjunction\\n1.(used to connect grammatically coordinate words, phrases, or clauses)\\nalong or together with; as well as; in addition to; besides; also; moreover\\npens and pencils\\n2.\\nadded to; plus\\n2 and 2 are 4\\n3.\\nthen\\nHe read for an hour and went to bed\\n4.\\nalso, at the same time\\nto sleep and dream\\n5.\\nthen again; repeatedly\\nHe coughed and coughed\\n6.\\n(used to imply different qualities in things having the same name)\\nThere are bargains and bargains, so watch out\\n7.(used to introduce a sentence, implying continuation)\\nalso; then\\nAnd then it happened\\n8. informal(used between two finite verbs)\\nto\\nTry and do it\\nCall and see if she\'s home yet\\n9.\\n(used to introduce a consequence or conditional result)\\nHe felt sick and decided to lie down for a while\\nSay one more word about it and I\'ll scream\\n10.\\nbut; on the contrary\\nI tried to run five miles and couldn\'t\\nThey said they were about to leave and then stayed for two more hours\\n11.\\n(used to connect alternatives )\\nHe felt that he was being forced to choose between his career and his family\\n12.\\n(used to introduce a comment on the preceding clause)\\nThey don\'t like each other—and with good reason\\n13. archaic\\nif\\nand you please\\nCompare an 2\\n14. See and so forth\\n15. See and so on\\nnoun\\n16.\\nan added condition, stipulation, detail, or particular\\nHe accepted the job, no ands or buts about it\\n17. See conjunction (sense 5b)\\nBoth and and but, and to a lesser extent or and so, are common as transitional words at the beginnings of sentences in all types of\\nspeech and writing: General Jackson thought the attack would come after darkness. And he was right. Any objection to this practice probably stems from the overuse of such sentences\\nby inexperienced writers. When one of these words begins a sentence or an independent\\nclause within a sentence, it is not followed by a comma unless the comma is one of\\na pair setting off a parenthetical element that follows: John is popular, and he seems to be well adjusted. But, appearances to the contrary,\\nhe is often depressed. See also and/or, et cetera, try.\\nMost material © 2005, 1997, 1991 by Penguin Random House LLC. Modified entries © 2019\\nby Penguin Random House LLC and HarperCollins Publishers Ltd\\nWord origin\\n[bef. 900; ME; OE and, ond; c. OS, OHG ant, OFris, Goth and, Icel and-; akin to G und, D en, Skt anti]\\nWord Frequency\\nAND in American English\\n(ænd)\\nnoun\\na Boolean operator that returns a positive result when both operands are positive\\nMost material © 2005, 1997, 1991 by Penguin Random House LLC. Modified entries © 2019\\nby Penguin Random House LLC and HarperCollins Publishers Ltd\\nExamples of \'and\' in a sentence\\nand\\nThese examples have been automatically selected and may contain sensitive content that does not reflect the opinions or policies of Collins, or its parent company HarperCollins.\\nWe welcome feedback: report an example sentence to the Collins team. Read more…\\nFailing health bodies will be named and shamed when the cancer data is officially released this month.\\nThe Sun (2016)\\nHe was found guilty and sentenced to another ten years yesterday.\\nThe Sun (2016)\\nIt has enabled us to try and stop people getting us into a cycle of aggravation.\\nTimes, Sunday Times (2016)\\nMercury is in your prize chart to add luck to lottery tickets and deliver exciting news for a family.\\nThe Sun (2017)\\nSailing courses and diving are also available.\\nTimes, Sunday Times (2006)\\nMost of it is made of cardboard and wood and still looks like a stage set.\\nThe Sun (2013)\\nThey are serving a life sentence of pain and misery.\\nThe Sun (2006)\\nThe beach is wide and backed with dunes and pine woods but the town itself has little of interest.\\nBrines, Callum Collins Traveller - The Algarve (1993)\\nDo a little dance, make a little love and get down tonight.\\nTimes, Sunday Times (2008)\\nGive the pavilion a sniff - it smells of tar and wood.\\nTimes, Sunday Times (2011)\\nand\\ninternational car registration letters\\nTrends of\\nand\\nView usage for:\\nIn other languages\\nand\\nBritish English: and / ænd; ənd; ən/ CONJUNCTION\\nYou use and to join two or more words or ideas.\\nShe and her husband have already gone.\\nAmerican English: and / ænd, ənd/\\nArabic: و\\nBrazilian Portuguese: e\\nChinese: 和\\nCroatian: i\\nCzech: a\\nDanish: og\\nDutch: en\\nEuropean Spanish: y\\nFinnish: ja\\nFrench: et\\nGerman: und\\nGreek: και\\nItalian: e\\nJapanese: ･･･と･･･\\nKorean: 그리고\\nNorwegian: og\\nPolish: i\\nEuropean Portuguese: e\\nRomanian: și\\nRussian: и\\nLatin American Spanish: y\\nSwedish: och\\nThai: และ\\nTurkish: ve\\nUkrainian: і\\nVietnamese: và từ nối\\nTranslate your text for free\\nBrowse alphabetically\\nand\\nancylo-\\nancylostomiasis\\nAncyra\\nand\\nand a half\\nand all\\nand all that\\nAll ENGLISH words that begin with \'A\'\\nRelated terms of\\nand\\nand co\\nand/or\\ngo and\\nand all\\nA and M\\nView more related words\\nSource\\nDefinition of and from the Collins English Dictionary\\nRead about the team of authors behind Collins Dictionaries.",\n  "url": "https://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/and"\n}\n**Web Page 6:**\n{\n  "context": "AND | meaning in the Cambridge English Dictionary\\nMeaning of and in English\\nand\\nconjunction\\nuk strong /ænd/weak /ənd/weak /ən/us strong /ænd/weak /ənd/weak /ən/\\nand conjunction (ALSO )\\nA1\\nused to join two words, phrases, parts of sentences, or related statements together:\\nAnn and Jim\\nboys and girls\\nknives and forks\\nWe were wet and tired.\\nWe kissed and hugged each other.\\nTidy up your room. And don\'t forget to make your bed!\\nand so on A2(also and so forth)\\ntogether with other similar things:\\nschools, colleges, and so on\\nand all\\nand everything else:\\nShe bought the whole lot - house, farm, horses, and all.\\nUK slang\\ntoo:\\nI\'d like some and all.\\nand all that informal\\nand everything related to the subject mentioned:\\nShe likes grammar and all that.\\nand/or\\nused to mean that either one of two things or both of them is possible:\\nMany pupils have extra classes in the evenings and/or at weekends.\\nMore examples\\nThe Australians have won three gold medals and two silvers in the swimming events.\\nIt costs a lot to feed and clothe five children.\\nSnakes and lizards are cold-blooded animals.\\nYour shoes will be repaired and ready for you to collect on Thursday.\\nI\'m afraid those days are gone and they\'ll never come again.\\nSMART Vocabulary: related words and phrases\\nWant to learn more?\\nImprove your vocabulary with English Vocabulary in Use from Cambridge.\\nLearn the words you need to communicate with confidence.\\nand conjunction (THEN )\\nA1\\nused to join two parts of a sentence, one part happening after the other part:\\nI got dressed and had my breakfast.\\nas a result:\\nBring the flowers into a warm room and they\'ll soon open.\\nStand over there and you\'ll be able to see it better.\\nA2\\nWith certain verbs, \\"and\\" can mean \\"in order to\\":\\nI asked him to go and find my glasses.\\nCome and see me tomorrow.\\nWait and see (= wait in order to see) what happens.\\ninformal Try and get (= try to get) some tickets for tonight\'s performance.\\nMore examples\\nSMART Vocabulary: related words and phrases\\nand conjunction (FOR EMPHASIS )\\nB1\\nIf \\"and\\" is used to join two words that are the same, it makes their meaning stronger:\\nShe spends hours and hours (= a very long time) on the phone.\\nThe sound grew louder and louder (= very loud).\\nWe laughed and laughed (= laughed a lot).\\nMore examples\\nSMART Vocabulary: related words and phrases\\nand conjunction (BUT )\\nused to express surprise:\\nYou\'re a vegetarian and you eat fish?\\nSMART Vocabulary: related words and phrases\\nGrammar\\nAnd\\nAnd is a coordinating conjunction. We use and to connect two words, phrases, clauses or prefixes together: …\\nGo and, come and\\nIn informal speaking we often use and after go and come before verbs like ask, buy, check, collect, do, find, get: …\\nFixed expressions with and\\nWe often use and in common fixed expressions. The order of the words cannot change: peace and quiet, pick and choose, come and go, knife and fork, black and white. …\\nAnd + adjective\\nIn informal speaking, when we want to emphasise something positive, we often use and after nice or lovely with another adjective: …\\nAnd in numbers\\nWhen we speak or write numbers, we use and to separate hundred, thousand, million from numbers smaller than a hundred: …\\nAdding with and\\nOne of the main uses of conjunctions is to add phrases and clauses together. The most common conjunction for adding is and: …\\n(Definition of and from the Cambridge Advanced Learner\'s Dictionary & Thesaurus © Cambridge University Press)\\nand | American Dictionary\\nand\\nconjunction\\nus/ænd, ənd/\\nand conjunction (ALSO )\\n(used to join two words, phrases, or parts of sentences) in addition to; also:\\nboys and girls\\nWe were tired and hungry.\\nAnd can be used when you add numbers:\\nThree and two are five.\\nand conjunction (THEN )\\n(used to join two parts of a sentence, one part happening after or because of the other part) after that; then:\\nI met Jonathan, and we went out for a cup of coffee.\\nand conjunction (TO )\\ninfml\\n(used after some verbs) to, or in order to:\\nLet’s try and get tickets for the hockey game tonight.\\nand conjunction (VERY )\\n(used to join two words, esp. two that are the same, to make their meaning stronger ):\\nThe sound grew louder and louder.\\nIdiom\\nand so on\\n(Definition of and from the Cambridge Academic Content Dictionary © Cambridge University Press)\\nTranslations of and\\nin Chinese (Traditional)\\n也, （用於連接兩個詞、片語、句子成分或相關語句）和，及，又，並, 然後…\\nSee more\\nin Chinese (Simplified)\\n也, （用于连接两个词、短语、句子成分或相关语句）和，及，又，并, 然后…\\nSee more\\nin Spanish\\ny, y entonces, y así…\\nSee more\\nin Portuguese\\ne, mais…\\nSee more\\nin more languages\\nNeed a translator?\\nGet a quick, free translation!\\nTranslator tool\\nWhat is the pronunciation of and?\\nBrowse\\nancillary\\nanconei\\nanconeus\\n-ancy\\nand\\nand all that jazz idiom\\nand another thing idiom\\nand co. idiom\\nand Company\\nTo top\\nContents",\n  "url": "https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/and"\n}\n**Web Page 7:**\n{\n  "context": "And Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster\\nand\\nconjunction\\nSave Word\\n\\\\ ən(d), (ˈ)an(d), usually ᵊn(d), after t, d , s or z; often ᵊm, after p or b; sometimes ᵊŋ, after k or g\\\\\\nDefinition of and\\n(Entry 1 of 2)\\n1 — used as a function word to indicate connection or addition especially of items within the same class or type — used to join sentence elements of the same grammatical rank or function\\n2 a — used as a function word to express logical modification, consequence, antithesis, or supplementary explanation\\nb — used as a function word to join one finite verb (such as go, come, try) to another so that together they are logically equivalent to an infinitive of purpose come and see me\\n3 obsolete : if\\n4 — used in logic to form a conjunction\\nand so forth\\\\ən-\u200bˈsō-\u200bˌfȯrth \\\\\\n1: and others or more of the same or similar kind\\n2: further in the same or similar manner\\n3: and the rest\\n4: and other things\\nand so on\\\\ən-\u200bˈsō-\u200bˌȯn , -\u200bˌän \\\\\\n: and so forth\\nAND\\nnoun\\n\\\\ ˈand\\\\\\nDefinition of AND (Entry 2 of 2)\\n: a logical operator that requires both of two inputs to be present or two conditions to be met for an output to be made or a statement to be executed\\nFrequently Asked Questions About and Example Sentences Learn More About and\\nFrequently Asked Questions About and\\nCan you start a sentence with and?\\nIt\'s perfectly acceptable to begin a sentence with and, as well as with other words that we are often taught to avoid at the start of a sentence, such as but and or. Examples of sentence-initial and date back more than 1,000 years.\\nWhat type of word is and?\\nAnd is a conjunction, and in particular a coordinating conjunction. Conjunctions are words that join together other words or groups of words, and coordinating conjunctions specifically connect words, phrases, and clauses that are of equal importance in the sentence. The other common coordinating conjunctions are or and but.\\nWhat does and mean in an address?\\nWhen and is used in describing locations, it indicates a point where two streets meet or cross. For example, in \\"I live at Third and Main,\\" the and indicates that the speaker lives close to where Third Street and Main Street meet or cross.\\nExamples of and in a Sentence\\nConjunction 2 and 2 equals 4.\\nFirst Known Use of and\\nConjunction\\nbefore the 12th century, in the meaning defined at sense 1\\nNoun\\n1946, in the meaning defined above\\nHistory and Etymology for and\\nConjunction\\nMiddle English, from Old English; akin to Old High German unti and\\nLearn More About and\\nTime Traveler for and\\nThe first known use of and was \\n                                before the 12th century\\nSee more words from the same century\\nFrom the Editors at Merriam-Webster\\nMerriam-Webster’s Great Big List of Words You Love to Hate\\nIs it Ever Okay to Start a Sentence With \'And\'?\\nDictionary Entries Near and\\nAND\\nand\\nanda\\nSee More Nearby Entries\\nStatistics for and\\nLook-up Popularity\\nTop 1% of words\\nCite this Entry\\n“And.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/and. Accessed 24 Apr. 2022.\\nStyle: MLA\\nSeen & Heard\\nPeople are talking about\\nMore Definitions for and\\nand\\nconjunction\\n\\\\ ənd, and\\\\\\nKids Definition of and\\n1: added to 2 and 2 make 4\\n2: as well as ice cream and cake strong and healthy\\n3 — used to describe an action that is repeated or that occurs for a long time The dog barked and barked.\\n4 — used to indicate the purpose of an action Please try and call.\\nand so forth\\n: and others or more of the same kindHe collects model cars, trains, planes, and so forth.\\nand so on\\n: and so forthYoung cats are called kittens, young dogs are puppies, .\\nTest Your Vocabulary\\nGreat Words for Scrabble\\nLove words? Need even more definitions?\\nSubscribe to America\'s largest dictionary and get thousands more definitions and advanced search—ad free!\\nMerriam-Webster unabridged",\n  "url": "https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/and"\n}\n**Web Page 8:**\n{\n  "context": "Does “and” mean multiplication or addition? - Quora\\nDoes “and” mean multiplication or addition?\\n4 Answers\\nBest\\nMichael Naunton\\n, former Managing Director on Wall St\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Author has 3.1K answers and 8.8M answer views\\nIt’s fairly reasonable to think of “and” as multiplication in a boolean system.\\nGiven “A and B,” where A and B are either true (non-zero) or false (0), “and” is multiplication. For example, the truth of “it’s raining and I’m wearing a red shirt and …” is multiplicative: I have to evaluate all the inputs to find if the statement is true.\\nGiven “A or B,” we get addition. “I’m rich or I’m good looking or I own a parrot….” is additive: I have to evaluate all the inputs to find if the statement is false.\\n2.4K views\\nView upvotes\\nQuora User\\n, Programmer (Of FORTRAN, COBOL, PL/I, BAL, & More) (1975-present)\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Upvoted by\\nDavid Joyce\\n, Ph.D. Mathematics, University of Pennsylvania (1979) · Author has 5.3K answers and 2M answer views\\nOne and One equals 2.\\n1 x 1 = 1\\n1 + 1 = 2\\nAddition.\\n2.7K views\\nView upvotes\\nAndy Wright\\n, former Maths Teacher for 20 Years, and Scientist Before T\\nAnswered 7 months ago · Author has 1.1K answers and 1M answer views\\n99% of the time, and means addition. The only way that I know it to imply multiplication is in probability. The chances of A and B, two independent outcomes occurring is P(A)xP(B), whereas the chances of A or B happening is P(A) + P(B). Of course, in this case, A and B need to me mutually exclusive as well as being independent\\n1.1K views\\nSteve Sawyer\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Author has 65 answers and 19K answer views\\nAs we begin to learn the number system, we are taught that two AND two are four. This is done for expediency. Later on we\'ll use more specific words to show exactly what we mean. The sum of 2 and 2 is 4. The difference between 6 and 3 is 3. The product of 2 and 4 is 8. The quotient of 8 divided by 4 is 2. In none of these cases does AND show an operation. It\'s simply links the numbers. Instead AND is used for an entirely different purpose. When reading numbers, AND is used to show a decimal place: e.g., “one thousand five hundred sixty seven AND 89 thousandths. Further, to avoid confusion, AND\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nAnonymous\\nAnswered 4 years ago\\nWhat is the difference between \\"or\\" and \\"and\\" in probability?\\n“or” means either event could take place. “and” means both events must take place.\\nFor example, let’s say there are 20 students who take only physics, 25 students who take only chemistry, and 15 students who take both physics and chemistry. The total number of students is 60. The probability that a student takes physics or chemistry is (20+25+15)/60, while the probability that a student takes physics and chemistry is 15/60.\\n33.9K views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nPhil Albert\\n, former Worked at NASA, So Have Stared Into Space at Work (1983-1986)\\nAnswered 7 years ago · Author has 6.6K answers and 3.5M answer views\\nAre there operators between addition and multiplication?\\nI think this is a great question.  It seems you want an operation that takes two numbers and the result rises faster than the sum of the two numbers rises, but not as fast as the product of the two numbers.  You can probably create an operator using some arbitrary rules that almost always meets those requirements, but that would be messy and would probably not be that interesting.  For example, max(x+y,min(max(x+y+1/x+1/y, xy-x-y),xy)) always falls between x+y and xy (unless my typing is in error).\\nThis question was posed earlier, in a different form (Is there any \\"partial\\" arithmetic operation\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nBill Andersen\\nAnswered 10 months ago · Author has 1.6K answers and 887.4K answer views\\nIs “per” a multiplication word?\\nOriginally Answered: Does \\"per\\" mean multiplication in math?\\n“Per” means division.\\nExample: Pens cost $6 per 12, how much for one? 6 / 12 = $0.50\\n44 views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nAlan Bustany\\n, Trinity Wrangler, 1977 IMO\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Upvoted by\\nBalázs Iván József\\n, Master Mathematics, Eötvös Loránd University (1983) · Author has 8.4K answers and 30.5M answer views\\nHow did we define addition and multiplication?\\nThere are many different ways to define the same underlying concepts but the two most familiar derive from the Dedekind-Peano axioms [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peano_axioms ] for the Natural numbers and the axiomatisation of the abstract algebra structure known as a Ring [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring_(mathematics) ]. Prior to these formalisations late in the nineteenth century we had largely intuitive definitions based on adding lengths and calculating areas.\\nThe Peano axioms depen...\\nUpgrade to Quora+ to access this answer\\nAccess millions more answer s like this\\nBrowse ad‑free\\nSupport the writers you like to hear from\\nStart free trial\\nLearn more\\n625 views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nJohn Hank\\n, studied Mathematics at Capital University (1968)\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Author has 858 answers and 227.6K answer views\\nIn English, does \'multiply by\' mean divide? And is it used \' Multiply with\' to mean multiplication?\\nMultiply by means to make multiple copies which are added together. It does not mean to divide. Examples: 3*7 means 7+7+7, and 7*3 means 3+3+3+3+3+3+3. However turning the numbers around is always the same answer. That is multiplication is commutative. xy = yx for all {x,y}. Divide is not commutative.\\n91 views\\nView upvotes\\nAnswer requested by\\nTofiq Khan\\nRelated Answer\\nMichel Paul\\n, taught mathematics\\nUpdated 1 year ago · Author has 642 answers and 633.2K answer views\\nIn higher mathematics, what is the difference between addition and multiplication (multiplication and addition of things that aren\'t numbers)?\\nStrange, the question I originally answered was What is the difference between addition and multiplication?\\nRegarding “things that aren’t numbers”, focusing on the terms ‘sum’ and ‘product’ is still useful.\\nAn interpretation of ‘sum’ and ‘product’ that does not involve numbers occurs in logic as the union and intersection of sets.\\nA ‘blue circle’, for example, is a logical product. It is the intersection of two sets, ‘blue’ and ‘circle’.\\nThe logical sum of the two sets would contain only elements that are ‘blue’ or ‘circular’.\\nWhat follows is my answer to the original question.\\n—————————————————————\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nVikas Goyal\\n, Student\\nAnswered 3 years ago\\nIf “$” means “addition”, “@” means “division”, “#” means “multiplication” and “%” means “subtraction”, then 72 @ 6 # 4 $ 12 % 10 =?\\nAccording to instruction the question will be as follows:\\n72÷6×4+12–10\\nSo the answer according to BODMAS is\\n72÷6×4+12–10=12×4+12–10\\n=48+12–10=60–10=50\\nSo ans. is 50\\n574 views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nAnwesha Mukherjee\\n, Ph.D. Statistics, Florida State University (2018)\\nAnswered 2 years ago\\nWhat is the meaning of \\"or\\" and \\"and\\" in probability?\\nLets start with two events A, B.\\nProbability of A or B occurring means-\\nOnly Event A happens\\nOnly Event B happens\\nEvents A and B both happens\\n(A and B) is therefore is a subset of (A or B).\\nNow you could extend this to ‘k’ events, k>2\\n1.2K views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nQuora User\\n, Electrical Engineer (2015-present)\\nAnswered 5 years ago · Author has 1.3K answers and 2.2M answer views\\nIf multiplication is repeated addition and exponentiation is repeated multiplication, is there anything that is close to repeated exponentiation?\\nYes, the operation is called Tetration, or hyper-4. And then you can repeat tetrations and end up with a pentation (hyper-5). And so on.\\nA general name for the sequence of operators addition, multiplication, exponentiation, tetration, pentation, … is hyperoperation (thus the ‘hyper’ in the alternative names above.)\\n621 views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nNishitha Nag\\nAnswered 4 years ago\\nWhy does \'\'and\\" means multiply and \\"or\\" means addition in probability chapter?\\nAND: Here 2 events occur at same time,so we multiply.They are disjoint events.\\nEg: Probability of getting odd number and less than 5 when a die is rolled.\\ngetting odd=3/6\\ngetting less than 5=4/6\\n=3/6×4/6=1/3.\\nOR: Here 2 events cannot occur at same time,so we add.They are independent events.\\nEg: Probability of getting 2 or 3 when a die is rolled =1/6+1/6.\\n6.2K views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nQuora User\\n, PhD candidate in medical imaging (MRI). Former high school math teacher.\\nAnswered 5 years ago · Author has 1.1K answers and 5.8M answer views\\nWhy are the addition and multiplication of the number \\"2\\" both the same (4)?\\nBecause adding a number to itself is equivalent to multiplying that number with two.\\nSo in order to have that ‘auto-addition’ yields the same as ‘auto-multiplication’ that number should be 2 (or zero).\\n555 views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nJohn O\'Donovan\\n, former Head of Department (Maths) (1978-2005)\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Author has 100 answers and 59.3K answer views\\nAre there single common terms for {\\"addition\\" and \\"subtraction\\"} vs {\\"multiplication\\" and “division\\"} that distinguish the former from the latter?\\nNot really. Though you could say that the former is just addition and the latter is just multiplication. Subtraction is just the addition of the additive inverse and division is just multiplying by the multiplicative inverse.\\nThus 8 - 3 can be thought of as 8 + (-3)\\nAnd 12 ÷ 4 is the same as 12 x (1/4)\\n405 views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nDouglas Magowan\\n, Private Pilot\\nAnswered 4 years ago · Author has and\\nWould it be correct if I were to say that multiplication is just a proportional addition?\\nI would say that multiplication is an operation that distributes over addition.\\nb=1+1+1+⋯+1 ⏟ b times a b=a(1+1+1+⋯+1 ⏟ b times)=a+a+a+⋯+a ⏟ b times\\nAnd that is all there is to define multiplication over the real numbers.\\n41 views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nAlan Bustany\\n, Trinity Wrangler, 1977 IMO\\nAnswered 5 years ago · Author has 8.4K answers and 30.5M answer views\\nWhat are the principles behind doing multiplication and division first, and then addition and subtraction?\\nThere are no principles. It is just a matter of convenience and convention.\\nMultiplication, division, addition, and subtraction are binary functions taking two numbers as arguments and returning another number, just like f(x,y)=z. Let’s call them m,d,a,s in place of f.\\nThe expression 2 × 3 − 8 ÷ 4+1=5 could be written:\\na(s(m(2,3),d(8,4)),1)=5\\nInstead for convenience we invented the infix notation (×,÷,+,− ). This immediately introduces ambiguity because x × y+z could mean either m(x,a(y,z)) or a(m(x,y),z), that is x ×(y+z) or (x × y)+z. One solution to resolve ambiguity\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nJaines Renn\\n, Degree in physics, research in photonics.\\nAnswered 5 years ago · Author has 237 answers and 235.3K answer views\\nWhat does multiplication mean in physics?\\nMultiplication, from a math point of view, is just rapid addition.\\nIn physics, math operations - of which multiplication is one - typically occurs between two or more Quantities:\\nFundamental (length[m], time[t], mass[kg], charge[C], etc) and/or\\nDerived (velocity[ m/s ], accel[ m/s 2 ], momentum[ k g m/s ], Force[ k g m/s 2,N ], Energy[ k g m 2/s 2 ], current[ C/s ,A, Amps], etc.)].\\nThe significance of multiplication (division) in physics comes from two ideas:\\nThat the values of derived quantities are proportional in some way to fundamental quantities. So if I was inventing the idea of Force, my experiments would sug\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nNicholas Cooper\\nAnswered 3 years ago · Upvoted by\\nDeepak Bakal\\n, M.Sc. Mathematics, University of Hyderabad and\\nDavid Joyce\\n, Ph.D. Mathematics, University of Pennsylvania (1979) · Author has 1.6K answers and 2M answer views\\nIf multiplication means repeated addition, then what does (-x)*(-y) mean?\\nMultiplication ONLY means repeated addition when working with the natural numbers. When working with integers, which include negatives, we no longer conclude that multiplication is repeated addition.\\nLikewise, addition is repeated incrementing ONLY when working with Natural Numbers, and exponentiation is repeated multiplication ONLY when working with Natural Numbers.\\nMathematics is about encoding things in a manner that can be described. With the integers, allowing negative numbers, we encode direction. Therefore, when multiplying with directions involved, we are saying that the first -x means t\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nAlan Bustany\\n, Trinity Wrangler, 1977 IMO\\nAnswered year\\nHow is multiplication related to addition?\\nThe fundamental relationship is that multiplication distributes [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distributive_property ] over addition on both sides. That is for all a,b,c:\\n* a ×(b+c)=(a × b)+(a × c); and\\n* (a+b)× c=(a × c)+(b × c)\\nAs a result of this relationship you can prove that multiplication is equivalent to repeated addition for the subset of elements that can be reached by repeated addition of the multiplicative identity....\\nUpgrade to Quora+ to access this answer\\nAccess millions more answer s like this\\nBrowse ad‑free\\nSupport the writers you like to hear from\\nStart free trial\\nLearn more\\n957 views\\nView upvotes\\nView 1 share\\nRelated Answer\\nTom McFarlane\\n, M.S. Mathematics, University of Washington (1994)\\nAnswered 4 years ago · Author has 2.6K answers and 4M answer views\\nWhy does putting two numbers side by side mean multiplication? Why not addition or subtraction or division? What makes multiplication so special?\\nInterpreting the juxtaposition of two numbers as multiplication closely reflects our natural language.\\n“Three x’s and four x’s makes seven x’s” directly translates to “3x+4x=7x.”\\nImagine if we had it the other way around, with addition as juxtaposition and multiplication written explicitly. We would then write “3*x4*x=7*x.” This would be a natural choice only if we said things like “Three times x four times x makes seven times x.” But we don’t say it that way. So, writing it that way is not the natural way to do it.\\nIn addition, the juxtaposition representing multiplication, and a larger separati\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nScott Robertson\\nAnswered 5 years ago\\nWhat would the link between addition and multiplication look like?\\nLet me seed a starting position.\\nWe have a pretend closed form function and need to connect this to the open Riemann Zeta Function. Assume this is a Real function that separates addition and multiplication (where AU is an additive unit and MU is a multiplicative unit) with a part half:\\nF(x)=1 2+A U,(1 2+M U),..\\nf(x)=∏(1 2+M U),(1 2+M U 2),(1 2+M U 3)\\nNow to make a “spoof” proof connect to the Riemann Zeta Function, one\\nEge Erdil\\n,\\nAnswered\\nWhat is the meaning of closed under addition, closed under multiplication?\\na S b S a+b ∈ S S\\nN Z Q R\\n(0,1)={x:x ∈ R,0<x<1} is closed under multiplication, but not addition. (0.6+0.7=1.3>1)\\nThe set of the half integers Z/2={x:∃ y ∈ Z(x=y/2)0.5 ⋅ 0.5=0.25 ∉ Z/2\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nAlan Bustany\\n, Trinity Wrangler, 1977 IMO\\nAnswered years ago\\nIf multiplication is repeated addition, how can one explain the result of multiplying two decimals, say 1.5 and 2.3?\\nBoth addition and multiplication are binary functions on an underlying set of numbers. The relationship between the two is fundamentally defined by the distributive property [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distributive_property ]. Multiplication distributes over addition — for all a,b,c:\\na ×(b+c)=(a × b)+(a × c)\\nIf there is a multiplicative identity [ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1 ], such as [math]1[/math...\\nUpgrade to Quora+ to access this answer\\nAccess millions more answer s like this\\nBrowse ad‑free\\nSupport the writers you like to hear from\\nStart free trial\\nLearn more\\n2.1K views\\nView upvotes\\nAnswer requested by\\nQuora User\\nRelated Answer\\nQuora User\\n, former Staff Member at Los Alamos National Laboratory (1989-2005)\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Author has 1.7K answers and 497.2K answer views\\nWhat is the mathematical explanation of \'multiplication\' and \'addition\'?\\nMultiplication and addition mathematically are any two operations that behave according to certain chosen axioms. In the usual arithmetic of integers, addition has certain properties: closure, addition of integers gives an integer; commutativity, A+B=B+A; associativity, (A+B)+C=A+(B+C); and some others. Multiplication obeys the above and the distributive law: A*(B+C)=A*B+A*C.\\nThere are other objects than integers that obey these laws (or some of them) which give rise to various areas of mathematical study.\\n58 views\\nRelated Answer\\nNicholas Cooper\\nAnswered 5 years ago · Author has 1.6K answers and 2M answer views\\nWhy aren\'t division and multiplication signs generally standardized unlike addition and subtraction signs?\\nTL;DR: Old keyboards and typewriters vs. human scribble.\\nThey technically are in modern texts, but not by using the signs that you mentioned. It is standard in technical writing to adjoin two terms to be multiplied, such as:\\n(1+y)(1+z)\\nThe reason that the operator × is omitted is typically because it is so very close in appearance to the letter x.\\nAs far as division goes, the standard is the solidus (fraction line), such as:\\n1+y 1+z\\nThis helps lead to standard (normal) forms that tend to se\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nTerry Moore\\n, M.Sc. Mathematics, University of Southampton (1968)\\nAnswered 3 years ago · Author has 11.7K answers and 9.9M answer views\\nWhy is it that only multiplication gives areas and not addition?\\nWhy is it that only multiplication gives areas and not addition?\\nAreas are given by addition.\\nA 2 by 1 rectangle can be divided into two 1 by 1 squares. The area is the sum of the areas of the squares. If it’s 20 by 1 we add the 20 squares. If it’s 20 by 10 we add ten 20 by 1 rectangles. Now this is equivalent to multiplying 20 by 10.\\nThe point is that areas are additive.\\nWhat about fractions? Work backwards. Divide the unit square into, for example, 100 small squares. Each must be 1/100 of the original square. Why? work the addition rule backwards.\\nMultiplication only works for rectangles anyway.\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nPrasanna Jayashankar\\nAnswered 5 years ago · Author has 279 answers and 128.5K answer views\\nWhy is multiplication first then subtraction and addition?\\nIf this is in reference to the BED AS rule then the answer is very simple.\\nIt is so by agreed upon conversations. The objective of a mathematical calculation is to lead to the same conclusion irrespective of the person evaluating it. In order for that to happen there needs to be an agreement as to the priority of operators when such operators or multiple operators are used in a single expression.\\nHas monkey an ass the red.\\nThe monkey has a red arse.\\nOr\\nHold him, don\'t let go.\\nDon\'t hold him, let go.\\nSame words different meaning\\n487 views\\nRelated Answer\\nArrad Rahman\\n, Full Time Student (2010-present)\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Author has 1.1K answers and 381.2K answer views\\nDoes difference mean multiplication or division?\\nDifference means minus or subtraction.\\nSum means addition.\\nProduct means multiplication.\\nQuotient means division.\\nHope this helps!\\n86 views Answer requested by\\nGautam Khanna\\nRelated Answer\\nRobert Nichols\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Author has 4.9K answers and 9.5M answer views\\nDoes multiplication always come before addition?\\nOriginally Answered: Does multiplication come before addition in an equation?\\nDoes multiplication come before addition in an equation?\\nYes, in the sense of performing multiplication before addition as in the order of operations, often signified by BODMAS/PEMDAS\\nIf you simplify: y = 6 + 4 x 3 Perform the multiplication first\\ny = 6 + 12 Then the addition\\ny =18\\n24 views\\nRelated Answer\\nAlan Bustany\\n, Trinity Wrangler, 1977 IMO\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Author has 8.4K answers and 30.5M answer views\\nWhat are the other fundamental operations in mathematics aside from addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division?\\nWhat do you mean by a \\"fundamental operation\\"? And do you mean Mathematics in all its glory or merely Arithmetic?\\nIf we restrict to just Arithmetic, the definition of the Field of Real numbers, ⟨ R,+,⋅ ⟩, has only two fundamental operators:\\n+ for addition; and\\n⋅ for multiplication.\\nA Field guarantees the existence of additive inverses and multiplicative inverses (except for zero, the additive identity), so the other two operations you mention are defined in terms of these inverses:\\nSubtraction a − b ≡ a+b ′ where b ′ is the additive inverse of b; and\\nDivision [math]a\\\\div b\\\\equiv a\\\\cdot\\\\[/math]\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nDana F Anderson\\n, Just interested in math is all. Kinda a hobby.\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Author has 9.3K answers and 4.1M answer views\\nWhat is the probability of addition and multiplication?\\nThat question makes no sense at all.\\nHere’s the best answer I can think of: addition exists, and multiplication exists. So the probability of either of them is 100%.\\n45 views\\nRelated Answer\\nWilliam Mccoy\\n, A former high school math teacher (Grades 9, 10, and 11). I have a Bachelor of Science Degree in Math Ed. (19…\\nAnswered 8 years ago · Author has 2.5K answers and 13.3M answer views\\nWhy are \\"factors\\" related to multiplication and not addition?\\n“ Factors ” are related to multiplication and not to addition because by definition “ factors ” are those numbers which when multiplied together give you another number called a product.  For example, the numbers 2 and 3 are factors of 6 because when 2 and 3 are multiplied together, the  product is 6, i.e., 2(3) = 6.\\nFor addition, on the other hand, the numbers that are added together to give another number, called a sum or total, are called addends.  For example, if you add 4 and 5 together, you get the sum 9, i.e., 4 + 5 = 9; therefore,  for this simple equation, the numbers 4 and 5 are each c\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nTanishq Chamoli\\n, B.E Computer Science Engineering, Chandigarh College of Engineering and Technology (2022)\\nAnswered 2 years ago\\nIs it true that multiplication is just an addition?\\nYes, it is.\\nWhenever we multiply 2 numbers\\na*b: In this we are going to add \'a\' with itself \'b\' times. Which will in the end give us the answer of a*b.\\nLet\'s use this for 2*3.\\n2*3 = 2+2+2 = 6\\n☺️\\n33 views Answer requested by\\nPrateek Jain\\nRelated Answer\\nAlbert Sheu\\n, worked at Quora\\nUpdated 11 years ago · Author has 330 answers and 2.1M answer views\\nHow long does integer multiplication take, as compared to addition?\\nThe answer to this question only goes into how long it takes for addition versus multiplication for specific languages and architectures. For the theoretical, asymptotic difference between the two operations, keep in mind that addition is O(n), and read more about multiplication here:\\nGiven two n-bit integers, what is the minimum asymptotic complexity, in terms of n, of determining the product?\\nThe amount of time it takes depends highly on the language, the CPU you are using, the state of the pipeline, and other factors, but I will try to describe some generalizations with adding versus multiply\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nRajdeep Ghosh\\n, 10+2 from St. Xaviers Collegiate School, Kolkata (2017)\\nAnswered 1 year ago · Author has 133 answers and 102.6K answer views\\nWhat is the meaning of “or” in probability?\\nIn regular problems in probability, a few general problem statements are encountered almost always.\\nOne such problem is “Find the probability of the occurrence of condition A or B”. This means that the union of the probabilities of the two conditions is the required probability.\\nThus when an “or” occurs in a problem with two or more conditions,\\nRequired probability=p(A U B)\\nNow if all the events are “independent” of each other in the previous situation,\\nRequired probability=p(A U B)=p(A)+p(B)\\nIf you encounter an “and” in the problem statement rather than “or”,\\nRequired probability=p(A Ո B)\\nThank you\\n845 views\\nView upvotes\\nRelated Answer\\nRajib Ghosal\\n, Being a techie, I always try to find technicality of Inter-Human Relationship.\\nAnswered 5 years ago · Author has 549 answers and 1.6M answer views\\nWhat is Addition?\\nIt is a primarily a mathematical process through which we get a sum of two numbers; for example, 1 ( one ) + 1 ( one ) = 2 ( two ).\\nWe also use this word in a sentence to provide more information in a statement; for example, Your consideration about the girl to be your friend is not true. In addition to that, she may harm your reputation if you do not check your step immediately.\\nFor etymological evolution of the word, please follow:\\naddition (n.)\\nlate 14c., \\"action of adding numbers;\\" c. 1400, \\"that which is added,\\" from Old French adition \\"increase, augmentation\\" (13c.), from Latin additionem (n\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nHarold Laski\\n, I was on my high school math team.\\nAnswered 3 years ago · Author has 260 answers and 335.1K answer views\\nIn probability, what is the difference between \'OR\' and \'AND\'?\\nHere are the two definitions as used in probability:\\n“OR” means that you are calculating the probability that either event A alone, event B alone or both events A and B occurred.\\n“AND” means that both events A and B have to occur.\\nFOr example, suppose that there are three coins, a penny, a dime and a quarter. in a bag and you have to take out two coins.\\nHere are the possibilities:\\nPD\\nPQ\\nDP\\nDQ\\nQP\\nQD\\nWhat is the probability that either one of the coins chosen is a penny OR a dime.\\nThe following fit the criteria of “OR”\\nPD\\nPQ\\nDP\\nDQ\\nQP\\nQD\\n100% probability\\nIn other words all of them because all choices contain either\\nContinue Reading\\nRelated Answer\\nPraveenkumar Kalikeri\\n, lives in Karnataka, India (1995-present)\\nAnswered 4 years ago · Author has 1.1K answers and 2M answer views\\nWhat is the meaning of multiplication?\\nMultiplication is the other name for repeated addition.\\n2 × 5=10\\n2 2 2 2 2 2+2+2+2+2 10\\n5+5=10\\n58\\nRelated Answer\\nWilliam Mccoy\\n, A former high school math teacher (Grades 9, 10, and 11). I have a Bachelor of Science Degree in Math Ed. (19…\\nAnswered 3 years ago · Author has 2.5K answers and 13.3M answer views\\nWhich number is neither prime nor composite?\\nOnly positive integers can be either prime numbers or composite numbers or neither; therefore, of the set of positive integers, only the number one (1) is neither a prime number nor a composite number. A prime number is a positive integer that is exactly divisible, i.e., a zero remainder, by exactly two positive integers: itself and 1. For example, 5 and 7 are prime numbers because each is exactly divisible by only two positive integers: itself and 1, i.e., 5/5 = 1 & 5/1 = 5 and 7/7 = 1 & 7/1 = 7. The prime numbers less than 30 are: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23, and 29. By the definition of\\nContinue Reading",\n  "url": "https://www.quora.com/Does-and-mean-multiplication-or-addition"\n}\n**Web Page 9:**\n{\n  "context": "AND | definition in the Cambridge English Dictionary\\nMeaning of and in English\\nand\\nconjunction\\nus strong /ænd/weak /ənd/weak /ən/uk strong /ænd/weak /ənd/weak /ən/\\nand conjunction (ALSO )\\nA1\\nused to join two words, phrases, parts of sentences, or related statements together:\\nAnn and Jim\\nboys and girls\\nknives and forks\\nWe were wet and tired.\\nWe kissed and hugged each other.\\nTidy up your room. And don\'t forget to make your bed!\\nand so on A2(also and so forth)\\ntogether with other similar things:\\nschools, colleges, and so on\\nand all\\nand everything else:\\nShe bought the whole lot - house, farm, horses, and all.\\nUK slang\\ntoo:\\nI\'d like some and all.\\nand all that informal\\nand everything related to the subject mentioned:\\nShe likes grammar and all that.\\nand/or\\nused to mean that either one of two things or both of them is possible:\\nMany students have extra classes in the evenings and/or on the weekends.\\nMore examples\\nThe Australians have won three gold medals and two silvers in the swimming events.\\nIt costs a lot to feed and clothe five children.\\nSnakes and lizards are cold-blooded animals.\\nYour shoes will be repaired and ready for you to collect on Thursday.\\nI\'m afraid those days are gone and they\'ll never come again.\\nSMART Vocabulary: related words and phrases\\nWant to learn more?\\nImprove your vocabulary with English Vocabulary in Use from Cambridge.\\nLearn the words you need to communicate with confidence.\\nand conjunction (THEN )\\nA1\\nused to join two parts of a sentence, one part happening after the other part:\\nI got dressed and had my breakfast.\\nas a result:\\nBring the flowers into a warm room and they\'ll soon open.\\nStand over there and you\'ll be able to see it better.\\nA2\\nWith certain verbs, \\"and\\" can mean \\"in order to.\\":\\nI asked him to go and find my glasses.\\nCome and see me tomorrow.\\nWait and see (= wait in order to see) what happens.\\ninformal Try and get (= try to get) some tickets for tonight\'s performance.\\nMore examples\\nSMART Vocabulary: related words and phrases\\nand conjunction (FOR EMPHASIS )\\nB1\\nIf \\"and\\" is used to join two words that are the same, it makes their meaning stronger:\\nShe spends hours and hours (= a very long time) on the phone.\\nThe sound grew louder and louder (= very loud).\\nWe laughed and laughed (= laughed a lot).\\nMore examples\\nSMART Vocabulary: related words and phrases\\nand conjunction (BUT )\\nused to express surprise:\\nYou\'re a vegetarian and you eat fish?\\nSMART Vocabulary: related words and phrases\\nGrammar\\nAnd\\nAnd is a coordinating conjunction. We use and to connect two words, phrases, clauses or prefixes together: …\\nGo and, come and\\nIn informal speaking we often use and after go and come before verbs like ask, buy, check, collect, do, find, get: …\\nFixed expressions with and\\nWe often use and in common fixed expressions. The order of the words cannot change: peace and quiet, pick and choose, come and go, knife and fork, black and white. …\\nAnd + adjective\\nIn informal speaking, when we want to emphasise something positive, we often use and after nice or lovely with another adjective: …\\nAnd in numbers\\nWhen we speak or write numbers, we use and to separate hundred, thousand, million from numbers smaller than a hundred: …\\nAdding with and\\nOne of the main uses of conjunctions is to add phrases and clauses together. The most common conjunction for adding is and: …\\n(Definition of and from the Cambridge Advanced Learner\'s Dictionary & Thesaurus © Cambridge University Press)\\nand | Intermediate English\\nand\\nconjunction\\nus/ænd, ənd/\\nand conjunction (ALSO )\\n(used to join two words, phrases, or parts of sentences) in addition to; also:\\nboys and girls\\nWe were tired and hungry.\\nAnd can be used when you add numbers:\\nThree and two are five.\\nand conjunction (THEN )\\n(used to join two parts of a sentence, one part happening after or because of the other part) after that; then:\\nI met Jonathan, and we went out for a cup of coffee.\\nand conjunction (TO )\\ninfml\\n(used after some verbs) to, or in order to:\\nLet’s try and get tickets for the hockey game tonight.\\nand conjunction (VERY )\\n(used to join two words, esp. two that are the same, to make their meaning stronger ):\\nThe sound grew louder and louder.\\nIdiom\\nand so on\\n(Definition of and from the Cambridge Academic Content Dictionary © Cambridge University Press)\\nTranslations of and\\nin Chinese (Traditional)\\n也, （用於連接兩個詞、片語、句子成分或相關語句）和，及，又，並, 然後…\\nSee more\\nin Chinese (Simplified)\\n也, （用于连接两个词、短语、句子成分或相关语句）和，及，又，并, 然后…\\nSee more\\nin Spanish\\ny, y entonces, y así…\\nSee more\\nin Portuguese\\ne, mais…\\nSee more\\nin more languages\\nNeed a translator?\\nGet a quick, free translation!\\nTranslator tool\\nWhat is the pronunciation of and?\\nBrowse\\nancillary\\nanconei\\nanconeus\\n-ancy\\nand\\nand all that jazz idiom\\nand another thing idiom\\nand co. idiom\\nand Company\\nTo top\\nContents",\n  "url": "https://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/english/and"\n}\n**Web Page 10:**\n{\n  "context": "and conjunction - Definition, pictures, pronunciation and usage notes | Oxford Advanced Learner\'s Dictionary at OxfordLearnersDictionaries.com\\nDefinition of and conjunction from the Oxford Advanced Learner\'s Dictionary\\nand\\nconjunction\\n/ənd/,/ən/,/n/,strong form /ænd/\\n/ənd/,/ən/,/n/,strong form /ænd/\\nused to connect words or parts of sentences\\n\u200b also; in addition to\\nbread and butter\\na table, two chairs and a desk\\nSue and I left early.\\nDo it slowly and carefully.\\nCan he read and write?\\nI cooked lunch. And I made a cake. (= you are emphasizing how much you have done)\\nWhen and is used in common phrases connecting two things or people that are closely linked, the determiner is not usually repeated before the second: a knife and fork • my father and mother , but a knife and a spoon • my father and my uncle .\\nQuestions about grammar and vocabulary?\\nFind the answers with Practical English Usage online, your indispensable guide to problems in English.\\nJoin us\\nJoin our community to access the latest language learning and assessment tips from Oxford University Press!\\nDefinitions on the go\\nLook up any word in the dictionary offline, anytime, anywhere with the Oxford Advanced Learner’s Dictionary app.\\nWant to learn more?\\nFind out which words work together and produce more natural sounding English with the Oxford Collocations Dictionary app.\\n\u200b then; following this\\nShe came in and took her coat off.\\n\u200b go, come, try, stay, etc. and used before a verb instead of to, to show purpose\\nGo and get me a pen please.\\nI\'ll come and see you soon.\\nWe stopped and bought some bread.\\nIn this structure try can only be used in the infinitive or to tell somebody what to do. Try and finish quickly.\\n\u200b used to introduce a comment or a question\\n‘We talked for hours.’ ‘And what did you decide?’\\n\u200b as a result\\nMiss another class and you\'ll fail.\\n\u200b added to synonym plus 1\\n5 and 5 makes 10.\\nWhat\'s 47 and 16?\\nWhen numbers (but not dates) are spoken, and is used between the hundreds and the figures that follow: 2\u2009264—two thousand, two hundred and sixty-four , but 1964—nineteen sixty-four .\\n\u200b used between repeated words to show that something is repeated or continuing\\nHe tried and tried but without success.\\nThe pain got worse and worse.\\n\u200b used between repeated words to show that there are important differences between things or people of the same kind\\nI like city life but there are cities and cities.\\nsee also and/or\\nWord Origin\\nOld English and, ond, of Germanic origin; related to Dutch en and German und.\\nSee and in the Oxford Advanced American Dictionary See and in the Oxford Learner\'s Dictionary of Academic English\\nCheck pronunciation: and",\n  "url": "https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/and"\n}\n\n\nNow you should analyze each web page and find helpful information based on the current search query "and" and previous reasoning steps.\n'}
